./runtime/transcripts/verifier/run-2026-07-27T16-57-05-165Z.jsonl:12:{"at":"2026-07-27T17:18:41.303Z","txid":"tx-s-002","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_XI9aUiS9rByQPWSBY6S2KqOH","name":"write_findings","arguments":{"edit_locus":"draft","locus_detail":"novel/chapters/ch-01/scenes/s-002.md","quote":"When I tilted it the typed line showed through the thin paper, blunt and plain: “DO NOT SELL THE IVORY REVOLVER. THIS IS YOUR LAST WARNING.”","contradicts_quote":"“Don’t touch anything until forensics are here, Ellis,” she said when she saw me.","contradicts_source":"novel/chapters/ch-01/scenes/s-001.md","canon_context":"Inspector Lane's instruction at the scene entrance: “Don’t touch anything until forensics are here, Ellis.” — novel/chapters/ch-01/scenes/s-001.md
(From the scene card and world rules: Lane warns Ellis not to handle evidence; earlier in s-001 she allowed close observation but explicitly forbade touching until forensics arrived.)","reasoning":"Ellis handles the sealed envelope (tilts it) after being explicitly warned not to touch evidence. The packet indicates Lane warned him not to touch anything until forensics, and though she sometimes permits limited observation, the draft gives no stronger permission here; the action therefore conflicts with the instruction as quoted in canon.","severity":"warning","subtype":"knowledge_contradictions","suggestion":"Either show Lane explicitly allowing Ellis to inspect the envelope (a line of permission immediately before he tilts it), or change Ellis’s action to a non-contact observation (hold the envelope up to the light without touching the flap, or read the visible typing through the envelope without handling it). Make the permission or the non-contact method explicit in the sentence that currently reads “When I tilted it...” so the action does not contradict Lane’s warning."}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_gS1dCXiFj7cbzdqnGe2CeQeq","name":"write_craft_finding","arguments":{"check":"thin_for_its_length","quote":"Anna Rowe stood in the doorway, practical and steady. She told me she had seen Mr. Harben at breakfast, just after eight, that Mrs. Harben had left for town a little after nine, and that Mr. Reed had called by later and left before eleven.

Iris Harben spoke to me in the small parlour off the foyer. She gave a neat account — she’d been in town that morning to discuss payments connected with the collection — and kept her voice level while she smoothed the edge of her hand against her wedding band.

Thomas Reed was in the service room, his hands carrying the faint grains of his work. He said he’d been in the workshop across the square until just before eleven and that a neighbour could confirm it.","reasoning":"The three interviews (Anna, Iris, Thomas) are presented as tight summaries rather than dramatized exchanges; each supplies useful information but the material is compressed to summary beats rather than scenes. At ~546 words the scene is already under target and these compressed interviews are where to add necessary depth without padding.","severity":"warning","suggestion":"Dramatize at least one of the interviews to carry the scene’s investigative weight. For example, expand Iris’s exchange into a short two-paragraph beat with a line of dialogue that shows reluctance about the envelope (she hems, then smooths her hand), or let Anna give a concrete sensory detail (the way Lucius held the note). Replace one of the summary sentences with a brief quoted exchange so the reader experiences the discovery rather than being told it."}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_OdY5qUrcpfcgUD93sOQmnE4e","name":"write_craft_finding","arguments":{"check":"inert_open_or_close","quote":"Lane watched from the doorway as I closed matters with Thomas and then returned to the study. She repeated, without irony, the warning she’d given me at the start: “Don’t touch anything until forensics are here, Ellis.” Her look after the words said I could look and ask questions, but I must not be a clumsy hand. I told her I understood. That would have to be enough for now.","reasoning":"The scene closes on a mood-of-resignation rather than a concrete next beat: Ellis accepts limitation and the scene ends. That leaves the reader without a clear action to follow and reduces momentum between scenes. It also misses an opportunity to set a precise next question or decision (for example, whether Ellis will press Lane for permission to inspect the weights or to follow a lead).","severity":"warning","suggestion":"End on a specific, active note. Replace the final sentence with a concrete next beat: e.g. Ellis deciding to inspect the clock weights if Lane permits (and therefore include the weights’ position), or Ellis pocketing a single scrap of evidence into an evidence envelope to hand to forensics later, or Ellis stepping into the foyer to ask Lane one pointed question. Any of these ties the close to the next scene and gives clear direction."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"yuanshi-sg","model":"gpt-5-mini","usage":{"input":7353,"output":4218,"cacheRead":9088,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3200,"totalTokens":20659,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785172673537,"responseId":"chatcmpl-E6JFa55BFMWJIJfjoP74KsuZJa5Gg","responseModel":"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07"}
./runtime/transcripts/orchestrator/run-2026-07-27T16-57-05-165Z.jsonl:2:{"at":"2026-07-27T16:58:12.857Z","txid":"tx-plan","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ApqXYL7evvaG8hVURkjgnuIj","name":"submit_plan","arguments":{"entities":[{"id":"char-ellis-fletcher","sketch":"Private detective and the story's first-person narrator. Mid-forties, methodical, wry, a former police investigator who left under a cloud."},{"id":"char-lucius-harben","sketch":"The victim. A reclusive horologist and collector of rare clocks and antique weapons; lived alone with a small staff at Harben House."},{"id":"char-iris-harben","sketch":"Lucius's wife. Younger than her husband, composed in public, financially and emotionally entangled in his collection and reputation."},{"id":"char-thomas-reed","sketch":"Lucius's former apprentice and business partner. Expert on clocks, brusque, had recent arguments with Lucius about a disputed sale."},{"id":"char-anna-rowe","sketch":"Housekeeper at Harben House. Practical, observant, the person who found the body and summoned the police."},{"id":"char-inspector-margot-lane","sketch":"Local police inspector assigned to the case. Respectful of Ellis but protective of procedure; cautious about letting a private eye meddle."},{"id":"loc-harben-study","sketch":"Lucius Harben's private study: a compact, well-appointed room lined with books and clocks, dominated by an ornate longcase clock and a display case of antique weapons."},{"id":"loc-harben-foyer","sketch":"The small foyer outside the study; the study's only door opens into this area. The rest of Harben House is silent and oak-paneled."},{"id":"obj-longcase-clock","sketch":"An ornate longcase (grandfather) clock, a prized item in Lucius's collection. It has visible hands, a striking mechanism, and an access panel in its case."},{"id":"obj-ivory-revolver","sketch":"A small antique single-shot revolver from Lucius's weapon display: ivory grips, gilded barrel. At the start of the case its status (in-place/missing/found) is a question to be resolved in the investigation."},{"id":"obj-brass-key","sketch":"A heavy brass key used for the study's lock. It is found in the study's lock when the room is discovered locked; custody and origin matter."},{"id":"obj-torn-note","sketch":"A scrap of paper found clutched in Lucius's hand with part of a sentence visible. Its provenance and whether it is genuine or planted is in question."},{"id":"obj-black-thread","sketch":"A length of thin black thread (waxed linen / monofilament) found tangled behind the clock — a small, apparently insignificant physical clue that ties into mechanical timing."},{"id":"obj-envelope","sketch":"A sealed envelope found on Lucius's desk containing a terse typed threat about an upcoming sale; the handwriting on the flap is later compared to suspects."}],"logline":"A private detective, called to a locked study where a reclusive horologist lies shot, must pry open a case where an ornate clock, a missing antique revolver, a torn note and a quiet household hide a carefully timed murder — and the closer he gets, the more someone tries to reset the hands of time.","narrative_person":"first person, char-ellis-fletcher","scenes":[{"intent":"Open on the locked-room discovery: Ellis arrives at Harben House, meets Inspector Lane and Anna, and is shown the study that is locked from the inside with Lucius Harben dead. Establish stakes, the room's layout (longcase clock, display case, desk), the brass key in the lock, the torn note in the victim's hand and the initial puzzle—no gun on display.","present":["char-ellis-fletcher","char-inspector-margot-lane","char-anna-rowe","char-lucius-harben","loc-harben-study","loc-harben-foyer","obj-longcase-clock","obj-brass-key","obj-torn-note","obj-ivory-revolver"]},{"intent":"Ellis inspects the study in detail: examines the clock (stopped at a specific minute), checks the weapon display (revolver missing or hidden), finds black thread behind the clock and the envelope on the desk. He interviews Iris Harben and Thomas Reed separately—collect their alibis, motives (financial quarrel, threatened sale), and small tells. Inspector Lane warns Ellis about upsetting the investigation but tacitly lets him proceed.","present":["char-ellis-fletcher","char-iris-harben","char-thomas-reed","char-inspector-margot-lane","char-anna-rowe","loc-harben-study","obj-longcase-clock","obj-ivory-revolver","obj-black-thread","obj-envelope"]},{"intent":"Ellis pursues a mechanical hypothesis: the clock's internal train could have been used as a timed trigger. He finds evidence of recent access to the clock's inner case and that the torn note was put in the victim's hand after death. Tensions rise when Ellis confronts Thomas, who admits to servicing the clock recently but denies any role in the death. An unexpected piece of evidence (a fingerprint or a peculiar crease in the envelope flap) points to Iris in a way that seems damning and believable.","present":["char-ellis-fletcher","char-thomas-reed","char-iris-harben","char-inspector-margot-lane","loc-harben-study","obj-longcase-clock","obj-black-thread","obj-torn-note","obj-envelope"]},{"intent":"Final scene: Ellis lays out the mechanics of the staged 'locked room' and exposes the murderer. He demonstrates how the clock and the black thread, together with a hidden placement of the ivory revolver inside the clock case, allowed someone to fire the weapon and then make the room look sealed. The twist: the person with motive and opportunity is not the obvious suspect; Iris engineered it to look like Thomas's specialized knowledge did the deed. The resolution ties the torn note and envelope into the motive, and leaves a hint that Ellis will not be content until he knows what became of a small piece of the collection.","present":["char-ellis-fletcher","char-iris-harben","char-thomas-reed","char-inspector-margot-lane","loc-harben-study","obj-longcase-clock","obj-ivory-revolver","obj-black-thread","obj-torn-note","obj-envelope"]}],"tense":"past","world_rules":["No supernatural elements or 'impossible' physics — every action must be explainable by real mechanisms or human trickery.","The narrator, Ellis, cannot know anything he didn't observe, be told, or reasonably infer; revelations must come by evidence or deduction shown in-scene.","Forensic details (gunshot residue, fingerprints, time of death signs) must be internally consistent and not contradicted later.","All significant objects, locations and characters used in scenes must be declared as entities above; later scenes may only refer to these ids.","Locked-room constraints are physical: doors and windows can only be manipulated by plausible mechanical means or realistic sleight, and any trick must be demonstrable within the story's scenes.","No retroactive retcons of committed facts; if a gap exists it must be acknowledged as such in the prose and can be left unresolved."]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"yuanshi-sg","model":"gpt-5-mini","usage":{"input":6403,"output":4509,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2688,"totalTokens":10912,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785171425214,"responseId":"chatcmpl-E6IvSolOoxfuRC5DWhE4ZFRdQOJer","responseModel":"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07"}
