./runtime/transcripts/writer/run-2026-07-27T16-57-05-165Z.jsonl:17:{"at":"2026-07-27T17:15:55.232Z","txid":"tx-s-002","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3TDyVK8z9dlOle4p9qkJyv7z","name":"write_staged_scene","arguments":{"prose":"I went back into the study with Lane’s caution still between us — a polite thing, like a hand hovering near a doorknob. She stepped inside, closed the door behind her, and said, quietly, not to the room but to me, “Look, Ellis, don’t upset anything. Be careful.” Her eyes held the rest of the sentence. I told her I would be; she nodded in a way that left me the job without saying so.

The clock dominated the far wall the way a stern man dominates a room: upright, carved, indifferent. The access panel at its rear was not merely ajar now; someone had worked in there recently. The latch that had sat crooked in my first look showed the faint smudge of grease on the brass where a thumb would have taken hold. The pendulum hung motionless; the hands were arrested at eleven seventeen. Two weights rested about halfway down the case, neither fully risen nor fallen — mid-height, as if the movement had been brought to a pause rather than allowed to run its course. The striking rack lay where it would when the mechanism had been drawn but not released.

Behind the movement, where the clock’s wooden frame narrowed to a carved bracket, a length of black thread caught on a small screw and tucked up behind the inner brass: “a black thread hung, waxed and stiff, caught on the inner bracket at the clock’s base, threading up behind the movement.” It was the sort of detail that pretends to be nothing and so becomes interesting; the thread had a faint line of dust above it and a cleaner patch below, as though hands had brushed the area recently. The base of the clock bore a disturbed dust pattern — an oval clean where a palm or a tool had rested — and the rear access panel’s edge showed the faint ridges of a screwdriver’s tip.

On Lucius Harben’s desk a sealed envelope sat as if waiting to be picked up. The flap had a peculiar crease, a shallow diagonal fold pressed across it so that it caught the light and made the paper look like someone had tried to seal it with a thumb instead of a finger. When I tilted it, the typed line inside was visible through the thin paper, the words as blunt as a stamp: “DO NOT SELL THE IVORY REVOLVER. THIS IS YOUR LAST WARNING.” The sentence sat on the desk like an accusation. I did not pry it open; forensics would want their say. The open space in the weapons case opposite the clock — the mount labelled IVORY REVOLVER — looked the same as before: an empty cradle, velvet crushed where a grip had lain.

Anna Rowe was in the doorway again when I turned. She was practical in a way that makes you trust the truth of her words. She told me, plainly, when she had last seen Mr. Harben: at breakfast, just after eight. She said Mrs. Harben left for the town at a little after nine; Mr. Reed had come by later and left before eleven. “He was in the workshop,” she said of Mr. Reed, “and then he was gone.” She folded her hands so nothing could do the worrying for her.

I spoke to Iris Harben in the small parlour off the foyer. She was efficient in conversation the way a book is efficient: spare sentences, everything in its place. She repeated that she’d been in town that morning to discuss payments connected to Lucius’s collection. Her voice did not waver; she kept smoothing the edge of her hand over the wedding band at her finger as she spoke, an absent motion that belied the steadiness of her words. She would not be drawn on whether the envelope had alarmed her; she said only that the Harbens had been approached about the sale and that it had made her anxious for the house’s finances.

Thomas Reed I found in the little service room, hands already dirty from work. He was brisk, impatient with the kind of question that wasted time. He told me he had been at the workshop, across the square, until just before eleven; he said a neighbour had seen him there. He kept rubbing the pad at his thumb and forefinger together the way a man who works with small gears does when he tries to steady himself. He spoke of the disputed sale like a wound: someone had claimed an object that he said belonged to him, and he had argued about it with Lucius in recent weeks.

Lane watched the two interviews from the doorway and then returned to the study. “I don’t want you lifting or moving anything until the scene is logged,” she said — and then, softer, “but take your eyes. Ask questions. Don’t be a clumsy hand, Ellis.” She had the manners of a woman who kept the law and the pragmatism of one who knew when the law needed a different kind of looking. I promised I would be careful. That promise would have to be good enough for the moment."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"yuanshi-sg","model":"gpt-5-mini","usage":{"input":17319,"output":2464,"cacheRead":1536,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1408,"totalTokens":21319,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785172489595,"responseId":"chatcmpl-E6JCcQSxS8LRk71Av6vpWe1HF3l7r","responseModel":"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07"}
